      1,
      "The AI Superpower Matrix and Verification Challenge",
      "Establishes Elton Boehnen's BEJSON ecosystem as an AI-native, zero-daemon relational database architecture by presenting the raw capability matrix and exposing how legacy enterprise JSON inflates cloud payloads by up to 95 percent. Provides copy-paste empirical verification prompt templates that challenge readers to pit Claude, Gemini, or local models directly against the raw codebase to independently verify speed, offline execution, and sub-50MB RAM claims. Concludes by challenging skeptical engineers to inspect the foundational byte layouts, setting up the structural breakdown of the legacy and modern format specifications.\n\nTarget length: about 150 words.",
      true,
      true,
      true,
      "",
      [],
      [],
      true
    ],
    [
      "814cf448-fbd7-4f3c-8b0a-bf76441c5bd1",
      2,
      "Architectural Evolution: The 104 Foundation and 105 Integrity Era",
      "Introduces the architectural progression from the legacy 104 format family into the 105 Integrity Era, explaining how immutable column-0 record UUIDs and per-field UUIDs bring cryptographic provenance to flat files without altering POSIX simplicity. Deconstructs the three structural variants across both eras\u2014the base homogeneous table (104/105), the primitive-only configuration manifest (104a/105a), and the multi-record single-file bundle (104db/105db)\u2014while defining the Multi-File Database (MFDB) strictly as an orchestration layer rather than a file format. Bridges into the mechanical core of the engine by previewing why decoupling metadata from raw rows unlocks massive token and runtime optimizations.\n\nTarget length: about 150 words.",
      true,
      true,
      true,
      "",
      [],
      [],
      true
    ],
    [
      "722b3f3a-2ea6-4dee-adac-718b10762d6c",
      3,
      "Positional Access, Field Separation, and Token Destruction",
      "Details the technical mechanics of positional access field separation, formally defining fields, values, and record rows to demonstrate how isolating metadata into a single top-level array eliminates repetitive key bloat. Contrasts single-typed tables with discriminator-based multi-typed bundles like 105db to show why structured matrix layouts mathematically eradicate token-weight hallucinations in large language model context windows while maintaining constant-time O(1) field-map memory lookups. Explains how this vector format achieves sub-50MB resident memory bounds on constrained edge hardware, paving the way for relational multi-table orchestration.\n\nTarget length: about 150 words.",
      false,
      true,
      false,
      "",
      [],
      [],
      true
    ],
    [
      "78bc6763-5d17-4a5c-9448-01066a11b89f",
      4,
      "Multi-File Database Orchestration and Cascade Referential Integrity",
      "Analyzes how the MFDB v1.40 engine federates up to millions of discrete 104 and 105 flat files under a root 104a manifest to deliver full relational database capabilities without spinning up a background server daemon. Explains the two-phase plan-then-commit traversal engine that enforces bidirectional manifest-entity handshakes and foreign key constraints across cascade, restrict, and set-null deletion rules with zero risk of circular lockups. Synthesizes the four-language cross-runtime parity guarantees across Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, and POSIX Bash, concluding with production integration patterns for the BEJSON Content Management System.\n\nTarget length: about 150 words.",
